    "title": "PILoT: an algorithmic instantiation (initialization, weight decay schedule) for controllable implicit sparsification",
    "content": "PILoT is a practical continuous-sparsification procedure that: (i) parameterizes weights as $x=m\\odot w$, (ii) applies weight decay $h(m,w)=\\sum_i(m_i^2+w_i^2)$ with a time-dependent coefficient $\\alpha_t$ (typically a decaying schedule with $\\alpha_t\\ge 0$, $\\int_0^\\infty\\alpha_s ds<\\infty$), and (iii) uses an initialization that fixes the per-coordinate scale $2u_{0,i}v_{0,i}$ to a chosen constant (e.g., $1$) across parameters. This combination yields a time-dependent Bregman potential $R_{a_t}$ that can be driven towards an $L_1$-like regime (where it approximates the $L_1$ norm) without requiring zero initialization, thereby providing a tunable implicit $L_1$-regularization while preserving convergence of the loss and the optimal interpolator selection property for underdetermined linear regression (the limit minimizes $R_{a_\\infty}$ among interpolators).",
